      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The reference class/base rate is the 14 monthly all-groups annual prints from Apr-2025 to May-2026, centred near the recent 4.0% level. Persistence is the selected time-series prior; no separate fitted trend or AR model is used. The same original all-groups annual-movement variant is used for every anchor and historical value; no seasonally adjusted or quarterly value is mixed in."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: A persistence prior starts from May's 4.0%. The 13 successive changes are -0.3, -0.2, +1.1, +0.2, +0.4, +0.2, -0.4, +0.4, 0.0, -0.1, +0.9, -0.4, and -0.2 percentage points; their sample standard deviation is sigma = 0.48 percentage points. July 2025's official original monthly increase was 1.3%; replacing that comparison with a 0.7% July 2026 monthly rise is a transparent -0.6 point mechanical base-effect scenario. May's -0.7% original monthly outcome supports a below-2025-spike July scenario, while electricity at 21.1% and housing, food, and services at 6.5%, 3.3%, and 3.7% respectively mean only a further -0.2 point net adjustment is taken for persistence rather than assuming broad disinflation. Thus 4.0 - 0.6 - 0.2 = 3.2%. The realized-dispersion interval method gives 1.28*sigma = 0.61 points, rounded to an implied 80% range of 2.6% to 3.8%."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Upside risk is a larger July annual electricity-price reset or renewed food and fuel pressure, which would land above the interval if it pushed the annual rate above 3.8%. Downside risk is a sharper reversal of the earlier rebate-related price level plus broad discretionary-price falls; a sufficiently large fall would land below the interval at under 2.6%."
      },
